---
import ArticleLayout from '../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import StoreButtons from '../components/StoreButtons.astro';
import { appStubNode } from '../data/schema';
import { SITE } from '../data/site';

// The iPhone landing page. It owns the exact-match /iphone URL and the "I Like
// Movies for iPhone" title, so it is the page that answers iPhone-searcher
// queries ("movie tracker for iPhone", "Letterboxd alternative for iPhone").
// It was an email waitlist while the app was in review; now the app is live on
// the App Store it is a real product page pointed at that intent.
//
// Schema: the shared appStubNode (same @id as the homepage app node, now
// operatingSystem "Android, iOS"), so this page reinforces one entity rather
// than minting a second, drifting app node.
const path = '/iphone';
const title = 'I Like Movies for iPhone';
const description =
  'I Like Movies is a free movie and TV tracker for iPhone, on the App Store. Track films and TV, decide what to watch with an AI assistant, and share one library, with no ads.';
---

<ArticleLayout
  title={`${title} | ${SITE.name}`}
  description={description}
  path={path}
  heading="I Like Movies for iPhone"
  jsonLd={[appStubNode]}
  disclosure={false}
>
  <p>
    I Like Movies is on the App Store, free, for iPhone. It is a movie and TV
    tracker built around deciding what to watch next: track films and series in
    one library down to the episode, keep private ratings and notes, and let an
    AI assistant answer "what should we watch tonight" in plain language. There
    are no ads and nothing to upgrade.
  </p>

  <div class="cta-row">
    <StoreButtons />
  </div>

  <h2>What you get on iPhone</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Films and TV in one library, tracked per episode, with a watchlist and private notes.</li>
    <li>An AI assistant you ask in plain words, filtered against what you have already seen.</li>
    <li>Monthly Discover collections built from your own ratings, not a popularity chart.</li>
    <li>Streaming availability for your country on every title, so you decide and find where to watch in one step.</li>
    <li>A household that shares one watchlist and one watched history with the people you live with.</li>
    <li>Import from Letterboxd or IMDb, so a library built over years comes across in a few taps.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>A Letterboxd alternative for iPhone, and more</h2>
  <p>
    If you have looked for a Letterboxd alternative for iPhone that also tracks
    TV, keeps your notes private, and shows where a title streams in your
    country, this is built for that. Films and TV live in the same library
    rather than in two apps, and the record is yours rather than a public feed.
    For how it compares to the apps you may be leaving, see the{' '}
    <a href="/guides">guides and comparisons</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions people ask</h2>
  <details>
    <summary>Is I Like Movies on the App Store?</summary>
    <p>
      Yes. I Like Movies is on the App Store for iPhone, free, with no ads. It
      is the same app as on Android, so a feature you read about on Google Play
      is here too.
    </p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>Does the iPhone app sync with Android?</summary>
    <p>
      Yes. Your library lives on the server, not the handset, so an account
      started on Android is already there when you sign in on an iPhone, and the
      other way around. A household shares one library across both platforms.
    </p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>Is there a Letterboxd alternative for iPhone that tracks TV?</summary>
    <p>
      I Like Movies tracks films and TV in one library, down to the episode,
      with private notes rather than public reviews, streaming availability per
      country, and an AI assistant. If a public review community is the point for
      you, Letterboxd is still the place for that; if a private tracker for both
      films and TV is, this is built for it.
    </p>
  </details>

  <h2>On Android?</h2>
  <p>
    The Android app is on <a href={SITE.playUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Play</a>{' '}
    too, free and without ads. Your library follows your account across both.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>

<style>
  .cta-row {
    margin: 0 0 2rem;
  }

  ul {
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
</style>
